+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+A merge left two rustdoc comment blocks detached from the functions they document. Rustdoc
+attaches a `///` block to the item that immediately follows it, so today those docs are bound to
+the wrong items (a function inserted above them during the merge), while the functions they were
+written for carry no documentation. This makes the generated docs mislead readers about which item
+each explanation describes.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- In `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs`: move the `addressable_scope` doc block (the
+  "ONE scope … / # Errors" block currently sitting directly above `repository_scope`) back to sit
+  directly above `fn addressable_scope`. The `repository_scope` doc block ("The same addressing
+  rule as [`addressable_scope`]…") stays above `repository_scope`. The two merged blocks are split
+  so each function documents only itself.
+- In `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs`: move the `synthesize_schema` doc block (the "Closed
+  like every other schema here…" block currently sitting directly above `assign_schema`) back to
+  sit directly above `fn synthesize_schema`. `assign_schema` will then carry no doc block of its own.
+
+Both edits move existing comment lines only. No function bodies, signatures, ordering, or
+whitespace formatting change, and no file besides these two is touched.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+<!-- None: no new system behavior is introduced. -->
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+<!-- None: this is a documentation-only repositioning; no requirement-level behavior changes. -->
+
+This change declares `skip_specs: true` — it alters only doc-comment placement, not behavior.
+
+## Impact
+
+- `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs` — two adjacent functions (`repository_scope`,
+  `addressable_scope`) get the correct doc blocks. No code change.
+- `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs` — `synthesize_schema` regains its intended doc block;
+  `assign_schema` loses the doc it was wrongly inheriting. No code change.
+- Regenerated rustdoc will now describe the correct items. No API, wire-format, dependency, or
+  runtime effect.
